ions of that time it was a  great time to be an idealist and yet  look what happened it was a great time  to believe in that we were going to find  it there was no polls saying to a  poverty war there was there was no  corporate support a public demand for a  war on poverty it was a great time to be  in this city  to wanting to try to make this a better  country and then all kinds of things  happen most of which I don't even  understand and I want to just reflect on  it and try to see if I can put our time  and our experience in the White House in  some kind of context of the of the  sixties it'll be a modest  book and it'll be three or four years  before we can I can get it done if by  then my credibility went up and down at  first there was euphoria that LBJ had  brought in this young man who didn't  know enough not to tell the truth and  then who got in trouble for telling the  truth and  begin to try to do what everybody else  does  and then I got into trouble we have said  our credibility was so bad we couldn't  believe our leaks it went up and down as  the fortunes as the fortunes of the of  events on the hill the election when we  lost all those seats in in in in  Congress in 66 and then of course the  war the war is the great great puzzle  the great great puzzle even to those of  us who some of us who were there at the  time President was deeply conflicted  about it from the very beginning there's  a record that he called me very early in  64 before the campaign and I said you  ought to take a vacation he said I can't  tell you occasion I gotta stay and worry  about that goddamn war I said what war  the war in Vietnam I mean I don't  understand it forty years later I  haven't thought much about it because I  don't know what to think about it or  what to say about it but it'll be that  my credibility you know was up and down  I do want to say Terry that that that  I'm a documentarian I take too long to  tell the story and I take too long to  speech but I promise you you'll have  your time all 30 minutes of it next  Tuesday next Monday night  Thank You mr. Moyers and I'd like to  thank you all for coming today and I'd  like to recognize students from the  Annandale high school's ap journalism  class and students from Deborah Academy  in Denver Colorado who are visiting us  with with us here today and I'd also  like to thank National Press Club staff  members Melinda Cook Pat Nelson Joanne  boos Melanie Abdo Dermot and Howard  Rothman for organizing today's luncheon  also thanks to the NPC library for the  research and thank you we are journey
 

